TITLE:: RLQuantale
summary:: The values a relation can take, and how they combine along a walk and between walks
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLRelation, Classes/RLGraph, Classes/RLCategory, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
A relation with values says more than yes or no about two objects: a cost, a strength. A quantale gives the two operations that composing relations needs: link::#-mul:: combines the values met along a walk, link::#-join:: chooses between walks. link::#-zero:: is "not related" (it absorbs mul and is neutral for join), link::#-unit:: the value of staying in place.

table::
## quantale || values || along a walk (mul) || between walks (join) || zero || unit
## code::RLQuantale.boolean:: || 0, 1 || and || or || 0 || 1
## code::RLQuantale.lawvere:: || 0 to inf || sum || min || inf || 0
## code::RLQuantale.multiplicative:: || 0 to 1 || product || max || 0 || 1
## code::RLQuantale.interval:: || 0 to 1 || min || max || 0 || 1
::

With the Lawvere quantale, composing relations is the min-plus product: the powers of a relation of costs hold the cheapest walks of each length, and link::Classes/RLRelation#-closure:: is the distance. With the interval quantale, a walk is as strong as its weakest step.

code::
RLQuantale.lawvere.mul(2, 3);        // 5: costs add along a walk
RLQuantale.lawvere.join(2, 3);       // 2: the cheaper walk wins
RLQuantale.interval.mul(0.5, 1);     // 0.5
RLQuantale.lawvere.leq(3, 2);        // true: a larger cost is below a smaller one
::

